#set text(12pt)


This is the top guy when talking about computer graphics. He was the pioneer of the transformation of scan lines and printed letters to the visual media we see everyday in our screens. He was born in May 16, 1938, in an engineer's house, where he became curious in the early parts of his life. He learned how to program when he was 15, which was very rare for the times. 

As for education, he didn't have to worry, as he was top of his class with great interest in the visual approaches to mathematical problems. He studied his bachelors in electrical engineering in Carnegie Mellon with a full scholarship, later he also went Caltech and after that, he studied his doctorate at MIT, where he published his thesis about _SketchPad_, the first program to manipulate visual objects.

_SketchPad_ was a big deal. Back then, computers used CRT monitors to output results of computations, but this program changed everything, it integrated the precursor of the stylus (in that time called 'Light pen') with which you could create you own drawings; along with new peripherals, he created the instance based modelling, which consists in a "master" object and copies of it, lastly he introduces constraints, which are conditions for the primitives being drawn (lines being parallel, angle constraints, etc.)

Also, he teached at several schools, including University of Utah and Harvard. At Harvard, he and his student created the first Head-Mounted Display (HMD) for Virtual and Augmented Reality. This device was nicknamed "The Sword of Damocles" because it required a heavy mechanical arm required to suspend it from the ceiling. It didn't just show a picture right in your eyes but it tracked the user's head movements and updated a 3D wireframe in real-time, effectively transporting the viewer's normal movements into a digital input space, this was decades before VR became mainstream.

Sutherland’s influence extended far beyond his own inventions through his role as a "master teacher" at the University of Utah. During the 1970s, he co-founded Evans & Sutherland and turned the university into a magnet for the brightest minds for computer science. Under his guidance, Edwin Catmull (who later co-founded Pixar) and John Warnock (the co-founder of Adobe) developed the algorithms for shading, hidden-surface removal, and digital curves that we still use today. Every Pixar movie, every Photoshop edit, and every 3D video game can be traced back to the environment Sutherland helped create.

He won the Turing Award (the "Nobel Prize of Computing") and the Kyoto Prize, which celebrated his work for making computers accessible to everyone. Even in his later years, he continues to push boundaries by continuing research in other input devices and microchip design. He has a famous stand: he says that he never did a day of "work" in his life—instead, he simply followed his curiosity, viewing the computer screen not as a cold machine, but as a "looking glass into a mathematical wonderland." 
